.TH WELCOME 1
.SH NAME
welcome \- a primer on Inferno: where things are, and how the place works
.SH DESCRIPTION
Welcome to Inferno. If you have just landed at a shell prompt or a
.IR wm (1)
desktop and you are wondering where everything is and how any of it fits
together, read on. This page is a short tour of the ideas the system is
built from. It is not a reference \- it points at the references \- but
after reading it the rest of the manual should make sense.
.SS Your programs are in /dis
There is no
.B PATH
environment variable. Every command is a file, and the commands live under
.BR /dis .
When you type
.B ls
the shell loads
.BR /dis/ls.dis ;
when you type
.B wm/vt
it loads
.BR /dis/wm/vt.dis .
A command name is simply a path, relative to the directories on the shell
variable
.BR $path ,
which defaults to
.RB ( /dis
.BR . ).
The trailing
.B .dis
is added for you.
.PP
So to see what you can run, just look:
.IP
.EX
ls /dis        # the everyday commands
ls /dis/wm     # the windowed applications
.EE
.PP
The
.B /dis
tree mirrors the source tree: the program written in
.B /appl/wm/vt.b
is compiled to
.BR /dis/wm/vt.dis .
Nothing is hidden in a magic bin directory; what you see in the file
system is what there is.
See
.IR sh (1)
for how the shell resolves and runs commands.
.SS Everything is a file, and the file system is yours to arrange
The deepest idea in Inferno is that
.I every
resource \- a device, a network connection, a running service, the
graphics display, even the set of environment variables \- is presented as
a small tree of files you read and write. There is no second mechanism. If
you can read and write files, you can drive the whole system.
.PP
Because resources are files, the interesting verb is not ``install'' but
.BR bind " and " mount .
You assemble the file tree your program sees \- its
.I name space
\- out of pieces. You can
.B bind
one directory over another, or
.B mount
a service (local or across the network) at a point in your tree, and from
then on reads and writes at that point are answered by whatever you
attached there. See
.IR bind (1),
.IR namespace (4),
and the primer in
.BR /docs/ON_NAMESPACE.md .
.PP
Crucially the name space is
.IR "per process" .
Each process inherits its parent's view but may change its own without
disturbing anyone else (see
.IR sys-pctl (2)).
A program can build a private, minimal world to run in. This is how
sandboxing, virtual hosting, and ``log in to that machine and use its
devices as if they were mine'' all fall out of one mechanism rather than
many.
.SS Styx: the file protocol is the network
The protocol that a process speaks to a file tree is called
.IR Styx " (also known as 9P; see " intro (5)).
The same protocol serves a local device and a remote service, so a file
tree on another machine can be mounted into yours and used exactly as if it
were local. Distribution is not a separate subsystem bolted on; it is the
file system working the way it already works. See
.IR mount (1)
and
.BR /docs/ON_9P.md .
.SS Limbo and Dis: write once, run on anything
Programs are written in
.IR Limbo " (" limbo (1)),
a small concurrent language, and compiled not to native code but to
.IR Dis ,
a machine-independent virtual machine. The same
.B .dis
file runs unchanged on every processor Inferno supports, and the virtual
machine executes it safely even on hardware without memory protection.
A just-in-time compiler turns hot code into native instructions where it
helps. The language and VM are introduced in
.BR /docs/ON_LIMBO.md " and " /docs/ON_DIS.md .
.SS Concurrency is cheap and built in
Limbo has processes and typed
.I channels
as language primitives, not library afterthoughts. You start a process with
.B spawn
and let processes talk by sending values over channels with
.BR <- .
The style throughout the system is many small communicating processes
rather than threads sharing memory. See
.IR sys-pctl (2)
and
.BR /docs/ON_CONCURRENCY.md .
.SS The shell is a programming language with a file system in it
.IR sh (1)
looks familiar but is not a Bourne clone. A value is a list; the shell
loads builtin
.I modules
on demand to extend itself \- arithmetic, regular expressions, even a
module that lets a script drive a
.IR Tk (9)
window or publish a file into the name space that the script itself
implements. See
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-tk (1),
.IR sh-file2chan (1),
and
.BR /docs/ON_SHELL.md .
.SS The desktop
.IR wm (1)
manages application windows;
.IR acme (1)
rolls an editor, shell, and window system into one. Graphics are drawn with
.IR draw (2)
and laid out with
.IR tk (2);
see
.BR /docs/ON_GRAPHICS.md .
.SH GOING DEEPER
For developers, the
.B /docs
tree holds the durable references, each a ``so you want to X'' topic guide.
A few starting points:
.IP
.EX
/docs/README.md          the front door
/docs/ON_NAMESPACE.md    name spaces, bind, mount
/docs/ON_SHELL.md        the shell in depth
/docs/ON_LIMBO.md        the language
/docs/ON_DIS.md          the virtual machine
/docs/ON_CONCURRENCY.md  processes and channels
/docs/ON_NETWORK.md      dialling, Styx, services
/docs/ON_GRAPHICS.md     Draw, Tk, and Prefab
/docs/ON_BUILDING.md     building and hacking on the system
/docs/ON_DEBUGGING.md    when a program misbehaves
.EE
.PP
There are more than thirty
.B ON_*.md
topic guides; list
.B /docs
to see them all.
